					<description><![CDATA[<p>Lumilens Inc. is known for providing optical networking chips for AI clusters. Recently, Lumilens raises $900 million to scale up and scale out. The bulk came from a $700 million</p>
<p>The post <a href="https://ventureburn.com/lumilens-900-million-funding-optical-chips-ai-data-centers/" data-wpel-link="internal">Lumilens Raises $900 Million To Build Optical Chips For AI Data Centers</a> appeared first on <a href="https://ventureburn.com" data-wpel-link="internal">Ventureburn</a>.</p>
]]></description>
										<content:encoded><![CDATA[<p><span style="font-weight: 400">Lumilens Inc. is known for providing optical networking chips for AI clusters. Recently, Lumilens raises $900 million to scale up and scale out. The bulk came from a </span><a href="https://lumilens.com/news-insights/lumilens-emerges-with-900m-in-funding" data-wpel-link="external" rel="external noopener noreferrer"><span style="font-weight: 400">$700 million Series C</span></a><span style="font-weight: 400"> that valued the two-year-old company at $5.51 billion. </span></p>
<p><span style="font-weight: 400">The round was jointly led by Atreides Management, Bain Capital Ventures, Meritech, Seligman Ventures and Spark Capital. Qualcomm and more than a dozen other investors also participated. The company is led by co-founder and CEO Ankur Singla. His last two networking startups, Contrail Systems and Volterra, were sold for a combined $676 million. </span></p>
<h2><span style="font-weight: 400">Why Data Centers are Leaving Copper for Optics</span></h2>
<p><span style="font-weight: 400">Historically, data centers used copper wires to move data between processors. That works at lower speeds, but AI clusters are pushing past those limits. At 1.6 terabits per second, copper can only go about one meter. That keeps you inside a single rack.</span></p>
<p><span style="font-weight: 400">Lumilens’ chips replace copper with fiber-optic cabling. Fiber can carry more data over longer distances with less power. For companies building thousand-GPU clusters, that’s becoming essential.</span></p>
<p><b><i>More Updates: </i></b><a href="https://ventureburn.com/google-ai-leadership-shuffle-hassabis-jeff-dean/" data-wpel-link="internal"><b><i>Google Shakes Up AI, Demis Steps Down As DeepMind CEO</i></b></a></p>
<h2><span style="font-weight: 400">Scale-up: Connecting Chips Inside the Rack</span></h2>
<div id="attachment_204132" style="width: 922px" class="wp-caption aligncenter"><img fetchpriority="high" decoding="async" aria-describedby="caption-attachment-204132" class="size-full wp-image-204132" src="https://ventureburn.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/08/Lumilens.jpg" alt="Macro photo of a Lumilens optical microchip on a server board with glowing light pathways connecting processors inside a modern AI facility." width="912" height="537" /><p id="caption-attachment-204132" class="wp-caption-text">Lumilens Photonic Chip</p></div>
<p><span style="font-weight: 400">Lumilens is targeting scale-up networking with two product families. First is Co-Packaged Optics, or CPO, that are integrated directly into GPUs. Because they sit closer to the processor, data travels a shorter distance and power efficiency goes up. </span></p>
<p><span style="font-weight: 400">Lumilens says CPO can link thousands of GPUs. Second is Near-Packaged Optics, or NPO. These sit on the motherboard next to the GPU instead of inside it. They’re less efficient than CPO but easier to deploy today.</span></p>
<p><span style="font-weight: 400">Signgla believes that we’re still about two to three years away when it comes to large-scale volume deployment of co-packaged optics. Lumilens taped out its first chip in 2024. Its second-generation chip is taped out and heading for commercial deployment next year.</span></p>
<p><b><i>More Updates: </i></b><a href="https://ventureburn.com/moove-250-million-to-lead-robotaxi-industry/" data-wpel-link="internal"><b><i>Moove Raises $250M To Lead the Robotaxi Industry</i></b></a></p>
<h2><span style="font-weight: 400">Scale-out: Connecting Racks Together</span></h2>
<p><span style="font-weight: 400">For scale-out networking, Lumilens makes chips for pluggable transceivers that attach to switches. These connect entire racks of GPUs.</span></p>
<p><span style="font-weight: 400">The technology supports 800 gigabits per second, 1.6 terabits per second, and beyond. Because it uses optics, it doesn’t have the distance limits that make copper impractical outside a rack. Singla said that in order to get outside of the rack, companies need to get to optics.</span></p>
<h2><span style="font-weight: 400">LumiCore and What’s Next</span></h2>
<p><span style="font-weight: 400">All Lumilens chips are built on LumiCore, a common set of building blocks. It includes photonic components, mixed-signal ICs, and interposers that act as the base layer for optical and electronic parts.</span></p>
<p><span style="font-weight: 400">The company says LumiCore cuts development time from years to months. The interposer design also makes assembly easier. Lumilens has built a custom manufacturing workflow with robots and AI software to support scale.</span></p>
<p><span style="font-weight: 400">Lumilens will use the $900 million funding to accelerate chip development and manufacturing. With AI labs racing to build bigger clusters, Lumilens is betting that optical networking becomes the backbone of next-gen AI infrastructure.</span></p>
<p><b><i>More Updates: </i></b><a href="https://ventureburn.com/hadrian-raises-1-37-billion-us-defense-production/" data-wpel-link="internal"><b><i>Hadrian Raises $1.37B for US Defense Production</i></b></a></p>
<p>The post <a href="https://ventureburn.com/lumilens-900-million-funding-optical-chips-ai-data-centers/" data-wpel-link="internal">Lumilens Raises $900 Million To Build Optical Chips For AI Data Centers</a> appeared first on <a href="https://ventureburn.com" data-wpel-link="internal">Ventureburn</a>.</p>
]]></content:encoded>
					
		
		
